  ],
  "text": "Astronauts sleep strapped to a wall and drink water recovered from their own urine. Everything up there is decided by two facts. Nothing falls, and nothing arrives cheaply. So water gets recycled. Sweat, breath and urine, back to drinking water, over and over. They recover well over ninety percent of it, because sending more up costs a fortune. And the body reacts exactly the way the physics says it should. Bone rebuilds to match load. With no load, it thins. Continuously. So they exercise about two hours a day, strapped to a machine, only to slow it down. Fluid stops pooling in the legs and moves to the head. Faces puff up and eyesight changes. And there is no down, so a dropped spoon does not land. It waits somewhere in the room. Nothing about it is exotic. It is your body responding correctly to a situation it never expected. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
